Chapter: UsbWalkPastTest
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 Two controllers, no disk on the first. A walk that takes the first controller
 that yields a disk must bring the second up, leaving ctl1 running; a walk
 pinned to ordinal 0 leaves ctl1 never opened. ctl1 carries no device in this
 bed, so connect=none is the right answer and the controller cells are the
 assertion. usb-walk-stop is the other half: with a disk on ctl0 the walk
 must stop there.

Section: Cells

  ctl-state : Integer -> Text
  ctl-state (ord) =
    let v = xdiag-get (xhci-ctl-base + ord * 4 + 2)
    in if v == xhci-ctl-running then "running"
    else if v == xhci-ctl-failed then "failed"
    else if v == 0 then "never-opened"
    else "cell=" & show v

  walk-line : Boolean -> Text
  walk-line (ok) =
    "connect=" & (if ok then "ok" else "none") & " seen=" & show (xdiag-get 40)

Section: Main

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing = act
    d <- msc-connect
    print-line-uni (walk-line (d.md-ok))
    print-line-uni ("ctl0 " & ctl-state 0 & ", ctl1 " & ctl-state 1)
  end
